# TestimonyArguments.SolaFide.WhatScriptureSays — the texts every later voice reads

Every later voice in this story, from the fathers to the Council of Trent to the
scholars of the last fifty years, is reading the same few pages. So the story
starts with those pages, grouped by writer, with as little comment as will let
a reader place them. How each later reader took them is for the chapters that
follow.

The texts here are quoted from the NET Bible, marked (NET). Where its wording
takes a side in a question the later chapters weigh, the Greek is given beside
it. Two such places recur. The NET renders Paul's verb δικαιόω as "declared
righteous" in Romans 3:28 and 4:5 and in Galatians 5:4, and as "justified" in
Galatians 2:16, Luke 18:14 and James 2:21–24; it is one Greek verb throughout,
and whether it means only declared righteous, or also made righteous, is the
question between the Reformers and Trent. And in Galatians 2:16 it gives
πίστεως Ἰησοῦ Χριστοῦ as "the faithfulness of Jesus Christ", Christ's own
faithfulness, where most translations and the Reformers read "faith in Jesus
Christ".

## Paul to the Galatians

Paul wrote to the churches of Galatia about the year 50, or a few years later.
Teachers had come after him and were urging his converts to be circumcised.
What exactly they meant by it is itself debated: the Reformers read it as a
condition added to faith, and James Dunn and N. T. Wright as a mark of who
belongs to God's people. Paul called it a different gospel:

> "I am astonished that you are so quickly deserting the one who called you by
> the grace of Christ and are following a different gospel" (Galatians 1:6,
> NET).

> "But even if we (or an angel from heaven) should preach a gospel contrary to
> the one we preached to you, let him be condemned to hell!" (1:8, NET).

"Contrary to" renders παρ' ὅ, which older translations give as "other than".

He told them how he had opposed Peter to his face at Antioch, when Peter drew
back from eating with Gentile believers, and went on:

> "no one is justified by the works of the law but by the faithfulness of Jesus
> Christ. And we have come to believe in Christ Jesus, so that we may be
> justified by the faithfulness of Christ and not by the works of the law,
> because by the works of the law no one will be justified" (2:16, NET).

Near the end of the letter he wrote:

> "Listen! I, Paul, tell you that if you let yourselves be circumcised, Christ
> will be of no benefit to you at all!" (5:2, NET). "You who are trying to be
> declared righteous by the law have been alienated from Christ; you have fallen
> away from grace!" (5:4, NET). "For in Christ Jesus neither circumcision nor
> uncircumcision carries any weight—the only thing that matters is faith working
> through love" (5:6, NET).

That last sentence, "faith working through love" (πίστις δι' ἀγάπης
ἐνεργουμένη), is one that the Reformers and the Council of Trent would both
claim. The chapter "Faith working through love" follows what each made of it.

## Paul to the Romans

A few years later, about 57, Paul wrote to a church he had never visited, and set
out his gospel at length.

> "For we consider that a person is declared righteous by faith apart from the
> works of the law" (Romans 3:28, NET).

He took Abraham as his example, the father of Israel:

> "Now to the one who works, his pay is not credited due to grace but due to
> obligation. But to the one who does not work, but believes in the one who
> declares the ungodly righteous, his faith is credited as righteousness" (4:4–5,
> NET).

And he asked when it was credited to Abraham: "Was he circumcised at the time,
or not? No, he was not circumcised but uncircumcised!" (4:10, NET). And of the
remnant of Israel chosen by grace, he wrote:

> "And if it is by grace, it is no longer by works, otherwise grace would no
> longer be grace" (11:6, NET).

## The letter to the Ephesians

The letter bears Paul's name. Those who hold it his date it to his imprisonment
in Rome, about 60 to 62; many others date it later in the century.

> "For by grace you are saved through faith, and this is not from yourselves, it
> is the gift of God; it is not from works, so that no one can boast" (Ephesians
> 2:8–9, NET).

## Jesus, in the Gospels

The Gospels were written down in the second half of the first century, and they
record what Jesus said. To the woman who wept over His feet in a Pharisee's
house, whose sins He had forgiven:

> "Your faith has saved you; go in peace" (Luke 7:50, NET).

He told a parable "to some who were confident that they were righteous and
looked down on everyone else" (18:9, NET).
A Pharisee in the temple thanked God that he fasted twice a week and gave tithes
of all he got.

> "The tax collector, however, stood far off and would not even look up to
> heaven, but beat his breast and said, 'God, be merciful to me, sinner that I
> am!' I tell you that this man went down to his home justified rather than the
> Pharisee" (Luke 18:13–14, NET).

When the crowd at Capernaum asked Him, "What must we do to accomplish the deeds
God requires?", He answered:

> "This is the deed God requires—to believe in the one whom he sent" (John 6:29,
> NET).

"Deed" and "deeds" render ἔργον and ἔργα, the word Paul and James use for
"works".

He said other things that the Council of Trent would quote. At the end of the
Sermon on the Mount:

> "Not everyone who says to me, 'Lord, Lord,' will enter into the kingdom of
> heaven—only the one who does the will of my Father in heaven" (Matthew 7:21,
> NET).

And to the rich young man who asked what good deed he must do to have eternal
life:

> "But if you want to enter into life, keep the commandments" (Matthew 19:17,
> NET).

## James

The letter of James has no date of its own. Douglas Moo dates it to the mid-40s,
before any letter of Paul's; others put it late in the century. James asks what
good it is "if someone claims to have faith but does not have works" (2:14,
NET), and does nothing for a brother or sister who "is poorly clothed and lacks
daily food" (2:15, NET).

> "So also faith, if it does not have works, is dead being by itself" (James
> 2:17, NET).

He too took Abraham as his example, at the binding of Isaac:

> "Was not Abraham our father justified by works when he offered Isaac his son
> on the altar?" (2:21, NET). "You see that a person is justified by works and
> not by faith alone" (2:24, NET).

This is the only verse in the New Testament where the words "faith alone"
(πίστεως μόνον) appear, and they are denied. The chapter "James 2:24" weighs
what James means by them.

## What comes next

Each chapter of the story that follows hears the voices of its own time, so
that a reply never comes before what it answers: first the church's readers of
Paul from the 350s to the 1270s, then the Reformers and their Catholic
opponents from 1521 to 1541, then the council itself in 1547, and then the
Reformers' answer to it, from 1547 to 1647.
